<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Balaklava</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Cembalo</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Balaklava is a former town, now part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sevastopol">Sevastopol</a>, on a narrow sheltered bay in the south-west of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimea">Crimea</a>. The Genoese held it as Cembalo from the 14th century until the Ottoman conquest in 1475, and the ruins of their fortress overlook the harbour. In 1854, during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimean-war">Crimean War</a>, it was the British base and gave its name to the battle that included the Charge of the Light <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brigade">Brigade</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/balaklava.jpg" alt="Balaklava" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: en: Denis Kamaev ru: Денис Камаев · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Modern_Balaklava_-_Gulf_of_Balaklava_(16.07.2006_17-10-20).JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
